-contained nosological terms that never absorb the gene name into their span. The proposed rule: bound the disease mention to the compact \"[gene/abbrev] + deficiency\" unit, excluding spelled-out expansions, parenthetical abbreviations, and \"of\"-PPs naming the protein, while adjectival \"deficient\" forms remain Modifier — noting that the boundary diagnosis for some examples is inferred since their gold spans weren't directly visible. I'll present this as Step 1 contrastive analysis followed by the single formatted Pattern Insight.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "**Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis**\n\n- **Contrast:** The True Positives are named, self-contained disease labels: \"Coats disease,\" \"Norrie disease,\" \"SLS,\" \"fragile X syndrome.\" Their spans end at the disease head, and the causal gene (NDP, FALDH, FMR1) sits outside the span. The Discrepancies are all \"deficiency\" nominals whose complement is a gene product, enzyme, or complement component: \"G6PD,\" \"C7,\" \"the seventh component of complement.\"\n- **Single separating feature:** Is the disease noun headed by *deficiency*, with a gene, protein, or enzyme as its complement? TPs: no. Discrepancies: yes.\n- **Isolate trigger:** The head noun *deficiency* combined with a protein-naming complement. This appears in two forms:\n  1. A spelled-out gene product plus a parenthetical abbreviation: \"glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase ( G6PD ) deficiency.\"\n  2. An *of*-PP naming a protein: \"deficiency of the seventh component of complement,\" \"deficiency of C7.\"\n- **Hypothesis check:** Both types are covered. Examples 1–2 show the gold type is already correct (SpecificDisease). In the same document, gold labels the compact \"G6PD deficiency\" (chars 772–787) as SpecificDisease and \"G6PD deficient\" as Modifier. So the model's type is right, and the error is the span: it expands the mention to the spelled-out name and the parenthetical. For Examples 3–4, gold and LLM agree on the type, so the discrepancy must lie in the span. The gold spans for doc 6859721 are not in the input, so this boundary diagnosis is an inference.\n\n**Step 2: Pattern Formulation**\n\n- **Pattern Insight**\n  - **Pattern Name:** Gene/Protein-Complement \"Deficiency\" Span Over-Extension\n  - **Confusion Trigger:** The head noun *deficiency* takes a gene, protein, or complement-component complement, either as a spelled-out name with a parenthetical abbreviation (\"glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase ( G6PD ) deficiency\") or as an *of*-PP (\"deficiency of the seventh component of complement,\" \"deficiency of C7\"). The model reads the whole string as one disease name and absorbs the molecular argument into the SpecificDisease span.\n  - **Contrastive Evidence:** True Positives never absorb a gene or protein argument into a disease span. \"Coats disease\" excludes \"NDP gene,\" \"fragile X syndrome\" excludes \"FMR1 gene,\" and \"SLS\" excludes \"FALDH gene.\" Their boundaries stop at the nosological head.\n  - **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** When a SpecificDisease candidate is headed by *deficiency* and its complement names a gene, protein, enzyme, or complement component:\n    1. Anchor the span on the compact disease-naming unit, [gene or abbreviation] + \"deficiency\" (e.g., \"G6PD deficiency\"). Exclude the spelled-out expansion of the abbreviation, its parenthetical, and any \"of\"-PP naming the protein, since these are referent expansions, not part of the disease label.\n    2. Keep the type SpecificDisease only if the phrase denotes the condition (a nominal state). If it is adjectival (\"G6PD deficient\"), label it Modifier.\n    3. Apply the same boundary rule to \"deficiency of [protein]\" constructions, and verify against gold spans before assuming a type error.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"epheme